I’ll inspect the diffraction data, metadata, and the supplied refinement utilities first, then screen candidate phases and build a staged GSAS-II refinement with branch-by-branch checks. I’ll keep the final exports tied to the audited `final.gpx`.

The chemistry strongly suggests zinc tungstate as the main reaction product, but I’ll treat residual ZnO/WO₃ and polymorph alternatives as live candidates rather than assuming a single phase. The scan contains 8,301 points and preserves the raw Cu Kα doublet, so the instrument model and Kα₂ handling will matter.

The peak families already point to at least two components: wolframite ZnWO₄ accounts for the dominant pattern, while the coherent extra set near 23.2°, 26.7°, 28.9°, 33.35°, and 34.3° is characteristic of WO₃. I’m now screening all database structures containing only Zn/W/O to distinguish residual WO₃ polymorphs and test whether ZnO has independent multi-peak support.

The structural audit rejected one superficially good WO₃ entry because it contains implausibly short O–O contacts, and another because of near-duplicate oxygen sites. I’m carrying the three best physically valid WO₃ models (monoclinic, triclinic, and orthorhombic) through matched refinements; a second valid monoclinic setting is included as a distinct replacement check.

The matched refinements favor low-symmetry WO₃ over the orthorhombic model. Triclinic WO₃ gives a marginally lower raw Rwp than monoclinic WO₃, but its six cell parameters oscillate with large shift/esd after repeated cycles; the monoclinic branch converges cleanly with nearly the same complete-pattern fit. I’m using the stable monoclinic model as the working baseline while retaining the triclinic result as a polymorph uncertainty check. Next I’m testing residual ZnO explicitly from that mature two-phase model.

The mandated angular test selected a specimen-displacement-only correction: Shift = −169.3 µm, reducing Rwp from 16.56% to 14.79%. The zero-only branch failed the stability criterion, and the joint zero/shift branch was rejected at 99.985% correlation. I’m retaining only the displacement term and will now test global line broadening and per-phase size/strain terms from this saved state.

The profile branches are still running in parallel; each is being judged on both fit improvement and convergence, with negative/unstable widths excluded. I’m testing Gaussian and Lorentzian terms separately so a broadening mechanism cannot earn credit merely by compensating another one.

The stable three-phase model remains ZnWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ + ZnO. I’m finishing the competing envelope/broadening tests now; the polarization branch already drove its parameter beyond the physical range, so it will not be retained despite its lower numerical Rwp.

The two-parameter roughness correction has now converged to a physically admissible solution (A≈0.165, B≈0.759) with only moderate final A/B correlation and broad, full-range benefit. I’m simplifying the remaining profile terms because the simultaneous V/W pair is more correlated than I want for the final model.

A cleaner envelope/profile model gives essentially the same fit with only V refined (Rwp 5.260% versus 5.246% for correlated V+W), so I’m retaining the smaller V-only model. Next I’m testing crystallite size and microstrain independently for all three phases, as required.

Only WO₃ shows material phase-specific broadening: isotropic microstrain is supported (≈3.7×10³ in GSAS-II units), while joint size+strain runs to the size boundary and is rejected. I’m now repeating the discarded broadening terms against this updated complete model before moving to texture.

The repeat screen confirms the smaller model: added W or axial divergence lowers Rwp only slightly but is strongly correlated with V or sample displacement; X/Z are negligible and Y is similarly correlated. No March–Dollase axis produced a material improvement, so texture is not retained.

The required angular recheck still selects the displacement-only baseline; the lower joint zero/displacement fit is rejected at 99.98% correlation. I’m now giving the triclinic, orthorhombic, and second monoclinic WO₃ alternatives the same mature nuisance model for a fair final structure comparison.

The mature replacement test favors the selected P2₁/c WO₃ model (Rwp 5.008%) over triclinic P-1 (5.044%); the orthorhombic and alternate monoclinic models are substantially worse. The mono/triclinic distinction is real but modest, so I’ll carry that as the main structural uncertainty. Leave-one-out confirmation is in progress.

Leave-one-out fits confirm every phase independently: omitting ZnWO₄, WO₃, or ZnO raises Rwp from 5.008% to 40.30%, 14.62%, or 6.70%, respectively. Removing displacement, V, roughness, or WO₃ microstrain also materially degrades the complete fit, so each retained optional term passes its matched removal test.

The deliverables are now exported from the selected project. I’m doing the final replay audit: regenerating the saved profile, checking every submitted CIF, and verifying that `result.json`, `fit.csv`, and `final.gpx` agree numerically.

Completed the three-phase Rietveld model:

- ZnWO₄ (wolframite, P2/c): 74.72(10) wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic, P2₁/c): 18.16(8) wt%
- ZnO (wurtzite, P6₃mc): 7.12(8) wt%

Final fit: Rwp 5.008%, Rp 3.812%, GOF 1.323. All CIF, GPX, profile-regeneration, and submission audits passed.

